\f0\b Self Care Plan \
 Antibiotics 
\f1\b0  treat a bacterial infection. Your child should feel better within 2 to 3 days after antibiotics are started. 
\f0\b Give your child his antibiotics until they are gone,
\f1\b0  unless your child's healthcare provider says to stop them. Your child may return to school 24 hours after he starts antibiotic medicine.  
\f0\b Acetaminophen (Tylenol) 
\f1\b0  decreases pain and fever. Ask how much to give your child and how often to give it. Follow directions. Acetaminophen can cause liver damage if not taken correctly.  
\f0\b NSAIDs
\f1\b0  , such as ibuprofen, help decrease swelling, pain, and fever. This medicine is available with or without a doctor's order. NSAIDs can cause stomach bleeding or kidney problems in certain people. Always read the medicine label and follow directions. 
\f0\b Do not give these medicines to children under 6 months of age without direction from your child's healthcare provider.
\f1\b0  
\f0\b Give your child plenty of liquids. 
\f1\b0  Liquids will help soothe your child's throat. Ask your child's healthcare provider how much liquid to give your child each day. Give your child warm or frozen liquids. Warm liquids include hot chocolate, sweetened tea, or soups. Frozen liquids include ice pops. Do not give your child acidic drinks such as orange juice, grapefruit juice, or lemonade. Acidic drinks can make your child's throat pain worse.  
\f0\b Change your child's toothbrush after he/she has taken antibiotic for 72 hours.
